hey there welcome back this is Simon Dev  where I code up random things of  interest things I've interests mostly to  me but maybe to you  mostly game related but really I just  like to program so if you have any ideas  for projects leave a comment below I  can't promise I'll actually do it but I  will at least consider and make an  effort  today's topic is genetic algorithms I'll  be going over those in detail and of  course I'll have some kind of pretty  pictures that I've generated along the  way let's dive in so what are they they  of course being genetic algorithms  because that's what we're talking about  today in this video anyway so what are  genetic algorithms you could think of  them as sort of a grab bag of assorted  tools that together are called genetic  algorithms this this grab bag of tools  is very very loosely modeled on  evolution so your intelligent design  people can can move along these are  inspired by evolution what do I mean by  inspired I mean that quite literally  they're simplification of the processes  that happen over the eons with evolution  so in evolution you have populations of  organisms that reproduce and survive and  thrive in given conditions and this is  what we're trying to replicate with  genetic algorithms what they do in  essence is a giant programmatic game of  warmer colder you know that game that  kids play where like so you have a ball  or a toy or something like that and you  go and hide it and the other kid puts  their hands over their eyes and they're  like they walk around and the other the  first one goes like warmer colder until  they find it kind of like I guess  another version would be like Marco Polo  in the pool hey apparently a Wikipedia  calls this hucklebuckle Beanstalk which  is the Haven even crazier name here hot  buttered beans I have no idea where that  name comes from that  that is a sweet name though and I will  go down that Wikipedia rabbit hole after  I finished recording this so back to  what they are so I said they're inspired  by evolution and what they do is they  sample a set of solutions and each  iteration they try and get a little bit  closer so going back to the biological  version you can think of a polar bear in  the Arctic and every successive  generation is better adapted to survive  in that climate so each iteration in a  genetic algorithm is trying to get a  little closer to the ideal solution and  most likely what you'll end up with is a  good solution like a good enough  solution not the best one because you  could imagine comparing it to  exhaustively searching every possible  permutation imagine for example that  you've got a function that takes one or  two variables you might be able to  exhaustively search through every  possible value for those one or two  variables but as that function gets more  complicated as it takes more variables  and the search space is larger that gets  prohibitively expensive very very fast  so with these super multi-dimensional  problems the chances of getting caught  in a local minima are pretty high and  the chances that the solution you end up  with is the global Maxima or minima are  super low like there are many many  possible good solutions and there's  probably only one perfect solution  genetic algorithms will in a reasonable  amount of time deliver a good enough  solution so how do they work like I said  grab bag of tools there's no one  specific way to implement them you  really just have a set of recommended  operators that you use and together you  call them a genetic algorithm so the  first step is to seed a random  population of solutions sort of  solutions with quotes around them  according with my fingers but I don't  have a webcam each of these being  essentially a guess at  answer next you need a way of ranking  them often this is called the fitness  function and where you go through each  member of your population and you see  how good of a solution it was and that  way you can sort everything from best to  worst after you have everything sorted  and you have all your best answers  you'll probably find that it's  especially in the first iteration that  all of them suck but a few of them will  suck just slightly less than the rest  and you'll work with those you'll select  those ones and start breeding them  together mixing and matching the  parameters to essentially create a whole  new set of guesses at the answer more  officially there are three operators to  use when breeding them together these  are called selection mutation and  crossover and selection is how you pick  which ones to breed mutation is how you  randomly permute the values and cross  over which is how you decide to mix the  parameters and these are actually quite  simple  some basic approaches would be to just  for the selection operator for example  you take the top 10% just cut off the  bottom 90% discard them and work with  the top 10% the mutation operator could  be equally simple so the mutation is  just going to randomly permute the  values a little bit so you just add a  little bit of random noise to it let's  say 1% of the range of the value and  then finally cross over and this is how  you decide to mix the parameters from  the two parents that you're breeding  together and simple approach it so that  would be to just randomly take values  from each one and finally once you've  done all that take all the new solutions  that you have or guesses and repeat  these steps and eventually you'll end up  with a pretty good answer like I said  probably not the best answer that you  can possibly have but a pretty decent  one so this has been all a bit academic  let's step through an actual example  here so here's a crazy obstacle course  that clearly I I drew myself there's a  big wall there and then I've got a  vote with maybe Gators in there I don't  know piranhas who knows and then at a  t-rex because you know this is this is  how I imagine what Tough Mudder would be  like so here at the start line you've  got your genetically engineered running  mutants or whatever they are and they're  all prepped to go now at this point it's  hard to know who's really going to excel  at this race it's the guy with the  Bigfoot is the guy with the oversized  head  who knows there's even one with like  giant arms and almost no feet we make  them race and some finish some don't  some get eaten by the t-rex or they  drown in the moat or they don't even  make it over the wall or who knows what  happened to them so now in this in this  example we're gonna take the top three  two of them finished one got really  close to the finish line for whatever  reason these three were better suited  than the rest for excelling at this  specific race and so we're gonna start  mixing and matching traits between them  and voila now you've got this totally  new set of runners a few of them have a  Bigfoot now a bunch of them have the big  head thing going on  one of them has both big head and  Bigfoot and we'll repeat this over and  over and over again until we're  satisfied that the set of runners that  we have is pretty good so let's see this  in action for real I went and downloaded  a few random images from the web and  I'll SiC my genetic algorithm  implementation on them and let's see how  this turns out  [Music]
 

